Title:
Defender Reversing – ProcOpen (Deepseek)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post reverse engineers why Microsoft Defender’s `MsMpEng.exe` opens a target process with `OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION | PROCESS_VM_READ)` during process start inspection. Using an ETW trace (Kernel-Audit-API-Calls) and a captured call stack, it walks through `mpengine.dll` functions leading to a “SyncProcessStart” path and a central classification routine (`MpClassifyProcess`). It breaks down decision gates that trigger “deep process inspection,” including image-name configuration lookups, IPM (image pattern matching), behavior monitoring (BM) verdicts, cloud/MAPS reputation checks, and special handling for LSASS/Protected Process Light. The deep inspection path is shown to query `NtQueryInformationProcess` classes `ProcessImageInformation (0x60)` and `ProcessSubsystemProcess (0x57)` after opening the process handle. The write-up is useful for Windows security researchers, red teamers, and defenders who want to understand Defender’s process introspection triggers and telemetry/ETW observables. It’s interesting because it ties a concrete ETW `OpenProcess` event to specific engine logic, feature flags, and inspection primitives.

Technical Focus:
- ETW tracing of Defender API calls (Kernel-Audit-API-Calls / `PspLogAuditOpenProcessEvent`)
- Reverse engineering `mpengine.dll` call stacks and dispatch/command routing
- Process classification pipeline (image allow/block config, IPM, PUA, BM/HIPS)
- Deep inspection triggers and caching (PID/command-line tracking)
- `OpenProcess(0x600)` usage and `NtQueryInformationProcess` (0x60 / 0x57)
- Cloud/MAPS reputation and telemetry/health-timer wrappers

Use Cases:
- Map Defender process-start scanning logic to observable ETW events and call stacks
- Identify which process attributes/contexts are likely to trigger Defender handle opens
- Build detections around Defender’s inspection behavior (e.g., `OpenProcess` + query classes)
- Guide reverse engineering workflows using Ghidra + MCP + LLM-assisted analysis
- Validate hypotheses about BM/HIPS/MAPS influence on process classification decisions

Keywords:
Microsoft Defender, MsMpEng.exe, mpengine.dll, OpenProcess, PROCESS_VM_READ, PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION, ETW, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Audit-API-Calls, PspLogAuditOpenProcessEvent, Ghidra, GhidraMCP, reverse engineering, ProcessScanEngine, SyncProcessStart, behavior monitoring, HIPS, MAPS, NtQueryInformationProcess, ProcessImageInformation, ProcessSubsystemProcess, LSASS, PPL, IPM Image Pattern Matching, telemetry, feature flags